也就是说，58岁的人认为：好吧，我完了，我定型了。我还是我，可能稍微胖一点，皱纹多一点，但我就是我。68岁的人回顾过去10年说：哇，我改变了多少啊。所以丹，真正令人着迷的是，无论我们在生活中经历多少次这些错误，无论我们犯多少次同样的错误，我们似乎都不会从中吸取教训，我们依然像以前一样自信，认为这一次，当我预测未来时，我会是对的。有两种错误。

25
00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:52.000
Sometimes you're driving some place and you take the wrong route. The next time you always get it right. But then there are errors that are more like, oh, optical illusions. You know, you see those two parallel lines on the page and you say, gosh, they don't look parallel to me and then you measure them and they are, but then you stand back and they don't look that way. So you measure them again, but they are, but they keep not looking that way. I think the kinds of errors we're talking about are a little bit more like optical illusions.
有时你开车去某个地方，走错了路。下一次你总是能走对。但还有一些错误更像是视错觉。你知道，你看到页面上的那两条平行线，你说：天哪，它们看起来不平行。然后你测量它们，它们确实是平行的，但当你退后一步看，它们看起来又不平行了。所以你再次测量它们，它们确实是平行的，但它们看起来依然不平行。我认为我们正在讨论的这类错误更像是视错觉。

26
00:12:52.000 --> 00:13:31.000
You can't help but see the future in this deficient way. Time and time again, you will find your predictions were wrong and time and time again, you'll make the same predictions. When we come back I'm going to ask Dan how we can use insights from his research to help us make decisions that are more likely to make us happier in the future. Stay with us. I asked Dan to tell me whether he's found ways to help people use their imaginations to make better decisions for their future selves.
你无法不以这种有缺陷的方式看待未来。一次又一次，你会发现你的预测是错误的，而一次又一次，你依然会做出同样的预测。当我们回来时，我要问丹，我们如何利用他的研究见解来帮助我们做出更有可能让我们在未来更快乐的决定。请继续收听。我请丹告诉我，他是否找到了帮助人们利用想象力为未来的自己做出更好决定的方法。

27
00:13:31.000 --> 00:14:03.000
We and many others have spent decades trying to figure out how can we make human imagination better. If when people look into the future, they make mistakes, what kind of training could we give them so they don't make these mistakes? And I will give you the summary of 15 years of research. It doesn't work. Almost nothing anybody has ever tried makes a long lasting broad difference. On the other hand, the best way to predict your future we've discovered is not to close your eyes and use your imagination. There is a completely different
我们和其他许多人花了数十年时间试图弄清楚如何让人的想象力变得更好。如果人们在展望未来时会犯错，我们能给他们什么样的训练，让他们不犯这些错误？我将给你总结15年的研究成果。这行不通。几乎没有人尝试过的方法能产生持久且广泛的差异。另一方面，我们发现预测未来的最好方法不是闭上眼睛使用你的想象力。有一种完全不同的

28
00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:29.000
discovered is not to close your eyes and use your imagination. There is a completely different way to figure out how you will feel. And that is to ask people who are already in your future how they feel. Almost anything you're imagining. Should I move to Hawaii? Should I become a trumpet player? I wonder how I should subscribe to the New York Times would it be better to have a bagel or a beale? Almost any decision you're debating, large or small, many people have already made it and they've made it in both
方法来弄清楚你会有什么感觉。那就是去问那些已经在你的未来的人，他们感觉如何。几乎任何你正在想象的事情。我应该搬到夏威夷吗？我应该成为一名小号手吗？我想知道我是否应该订阅《纽约时报》，吃百吉饼还是吃比尔饼会更好？几乎任何你正在争论的决定，无论大小，许多人已经做出了决定，而且他们已经做出了

29
00:14:29.000 --> 00:15:10.000
directions. There are people who are doing the things you're only imagining. And it turns out that if you can simply measure their happiness, you can get a pretty good guide about how happy you will be in the future. One of the experiments that we did that demonstrated this most simply is we created a speed dating clinic where young women came in and they had a speed date with a young man. And they were offered one of two kinds of information. Either they could learn all about the young man before they met him, they could see his photograph, learn his hobbies, find out about his likes and his dislikes, or they could learn none of that and they could get just one piece of information, namely, how much did the woman who already had
两个方向的决定。有些人正在做你只是在想象的事情。事实证明，如果你能简单地衡量他们的幸福感，你就能得到一个关于你未来会有多快乐的很好的指南。我们所做的最简单地证明这一点的实验之一是，我们创建了一个快速约会诊所，年轻女性进来，与一名年轻男子进行快速约会。她们被提供了两种信息中的一种。要么她们可以在见面之前了解关于这个年轻男子的所有信息，她们可以看到他的照片，了解他的爱好，找出他的喜好和厌恶，要么她们可以什么都不了解，只得到一条信息，即已经和他

30
00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:42.000
a speed date with him enjoy it? Well, it turns out, first of all, everybody wants the first kind of information. Women in our study all think they're going to make a much better prediction of how much they'll enjoy the date if they can just learn about the guy. But what we find is that they're much more accurate in predicting their own experience if they know nothing except another woman's experience. This woman is in a sense a surrogate for their future selves and we call this method surrogation using other people's experiences a guide to your own.
进行过快速约会的女性有多享受这次约会？嗯，事实证明，首先，每个人都想要第一种信息。我们研究中的女性都认为，如果她们能了解这个男人，她们就能更准确地预测自己会有多享受这次约会。但我们发现，如果她们除了另一个女性的经历之外什么都不知道，她们在预测自己的体验时会准确得多。这个女性在某种意义上是她们未来自己的代理人，我们称这种方法为代理法，即利用他人的经历作为你自己的指南。

31
00:15:42.000 --> 00:16:04.000
So there's a commercial application basically is built on this idea. It's called YEP. Exactly right. There are a few domains in which we all know that we want to rely on other people's reviews, if you will. Movies and restaurants, those are the two big domains. Now why are we willing to rely on other people in these domains? Because we can't imagine for ourselves.
所以基本上有一个商业应用是建立在这个想法上的。它叫Yelp。完全正确。我们都知道在少数几个领域，我们想要依赖他人的评论。电影和餐馆，这是两个大领域。现在，为什么我们愿意在这些领域依赖他人？因为我们无法为自己想象。

32
00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:34.000
I mean, if I say how much do you think you'll enjoy the new restaurant, you look at me and go, how can I possibly close my eyes and imagine that? When we have no information about a future event, we become surrogators, we're willing to listen to other people. But if you offer folks, even the smallest piece of information about a future event, they will take that over all the Yelp reviews. So let me ask you, you're going to decide whether to spend a lot of money to buy a movie. Would you rather see the trailer or would you rather read reviews?
我的意思是，如果我问你觉得你会多享受这家新餐馆，你看着我会说：我怎么可能闭上眼睛想象那个？当我们对未来事件没有任何信息时，我们就会成为代理人，我们愿意听取他人的意见。但如果你给人们提供关于未来事件的哪怕最小的一点信息，他们也会选择那个信息，而不是所有的Yelp评论。所以让我问你，你要决定是否花很多钱买一部电影。你宁愿看预告片还是宁愿读评论？

33
00:16:34.000 --> 00:17:09.000
If you're like most people, you would throw out all the reviews by every critic and every Yelp earned the world if you could just see the trailer. Because you trust your own judgment in some ways better than others. You feel if you could just taste 30 seconds, 60 seconds of the movie, you'll know if it's the kind of movie you like, but you won't. Other people's reviews are going to be great information about things like, did the movie go on too long? Did the plot get predictable? Did God come out of a machine in the end? All sorts of things you'll never get from a trailer. And yet, if you offer people these two kinds of information, the trailer every time.
如果你像大多数人一样，只要能看预告片，你就会扔掉世界上所有评论家和Yelp用户的评论。因为你在某种程度上比别人更信任自己的判断。你觉得如果你能品尝30秒、60秒的电影，你就会知道这是否是你喜欢的电影类型，但你不会知道。他人的评论将是关于诸如“电影是否太长了？”、“情节是否变得可预测？”、“最后是否有神来之笔？”等各种你永远无法从预告片中得到的信息的绝佳来源。然而，如果你给人们提供这两种信息，他们每次都会选择预告片。

34
00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:40.000
So would you suggest that we should actually have Yelp for everything, that we should be rating our friends and spouses and children and professors, all via this kind of distributed review system? Well, as a professor, I do get rated already. So there is a review system for me. And, you know, at Harvard, we have a quaint tradition. It's called Shopping Week. And in the first week of school, all the students come in and they visit classes. They don't choose their classes ahead of time. They visit them all to see 15 or 20 minutes of a lecture
所以你会建议我们实际上应该为所有事情建立Yelp，我们应该通过这种分布式评论系统来评价我们的朋友、配偶、孩子和教授吗？嗯，作为一名教授，我已经得到了评价。所以确实有一个针对我的评价系统。而且，你知道，在哈佛，我们有一个古老的传统。它叫“购物周”。在开学的第一周，所有的学生都会进来参观课程。他们不会提前选择课程。他们会参观所有的课程，看15或20分钟的讲座，

35
00:17:40.000 --> 00:18:09.000
because they're convinced, and the administration is convinced, that they can tell from 20 minutes of someone's lecture whether they'll really enjoy and get a lot out of the class. What they're ignoring is a huge database of information from people who actually sat through the class for 15 weeks and have a whole lot to say about whether you'll enjoy it or not. You can't convince a student to take a class that has great reviews if they didn't like the 10 minutes of the first lecture. The problem is something we call the illusion of diversity.
因为他们确信，行政部门也确信，他们可以通过某人20分钟的讲座判断他们是否真的会享受并从这门课中获得很多东西。他们忽略的是一个巨大的信息数据库，来自那些真正坐下来听了15周课程的人，他们对你是否会享受这门课有很多话要说。如果学生不喜欢第一节课的10分钟，你就无法说服他们去上一门评价很高的课。问题在于我们所说的“多样性错觉”。

36
00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:42.000
We think we are utterly unique that other people's experiences might tell us a little bit about ours, but not very much because after all, we're so different than everybody. Well nonsense, you're not different than everybody, we're basically all the same. You know, if a Martian knew everything there was to know, everything there was to know about a single human being on Earth, that Martian would know 90% of what there is to know about everybody on Earth. I have to push back just a little bit, because there's also been, I think, a voluminous research conducted by maybe colleagues in the psychology department, which finds that these reviews are
我们认为我们是完全独特的，他人的经历可能告诉我们一点关于我们自己的信息，但不会太多，因为毕竟，我们和每个人都如此不同。嗯，胡说八道，你并不比每个人都不同，我们基本上都是一样的。你知道，如果一个火星人知道关于地球上某一个人类所能知道的一切，那个火星人就会知道关于地球上每个人90%的信息。我必须稍微反驳一下，因为我认为，也许心理学系的同事们也进行了大量的研究，发现这些评论

37
00:18:42.000 --> 00:19:12.000
also shot through with all kinds of biases. So female professors, for example, get much worse reviews than male professors when they're just as good. I mean, isn't there a risk in essentially outsourcing your judgment to the crowd when the crowd actually is capable fully of having, you know, collective biases? Well, two things to say about this first, if you share the biases of the reviewers, they will correctly predict how you will feel, even if it's unfair. So let's say you just don't like old guys like me.
也充满了各种偏见。例如，女性教授在表现同样出色时，得到的评价却比男性教授差得多。我的意思是，当群体完全有能力拥有集体偏见时，将你的判断外包给群体难道没有风险吗？嗯，关于这一点有两件事要说：首先，如果你和评论者有相同的偏见，他们会准确地预测你的感受，即使这是不公平的。所以假设你就是不喜欢像我这样的老家伙。

38
00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:29.000
Let's say there's a bias. Students can't stand to hear a guy in his 50s, Yammer and Yammer. Well, that's not very fair, that's Aegis, that's wrong. On the other hand, when the students say I hated it, it's a pretty good guide for another 18-year-old who will probably hate it for all the same reasons. The second point I'd make is this,
假设存在一种偏见。学生们无法忍受听一个50多岁的人喋喋不休。嗯，那不太公平，那是年龄歧视，那是错误的。另一方面，当学生们说“我讨厌它”时，对于另一个可能因为同样原因而讨厌它的18岁年轻人来说，这是一个很好的指南。我要说的第二点是，

39
00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:51.000
I'm not saying that surrogation is a perfect way to make decisions. You know, Winston Churchill once said, democracy is the worst form of government except for the others. Well, surrogation is a terrible way to make predictions about your own future happiness, except for the other way to make predictions. You know, imagination is so utterly flawed that surrogation, even with all of its problems,
我并不是说代理法是做决定的完美方式。你知道，温斯顿·丘吉尔曾经说过，民主是最糟糕的政府形式，除了其他所有形式。嗯，代理法是一种预测你自己未来幸福感的糟糕方式，除了其他预测方式之外。你知道，想象力是如此彻底地有缺陷，以至于代理法，尽管有所有的问题，

